The function should return, in sorted order, the keys present in all three dicts d1, d2 and d3, and an empty list if no key is shared by all three.

The first definition computes the wrong set operation, and a later definition wraps it with a lookup of the example arguments:

    def common_keys_three(d1, d2, d3):
        return sorted(set(d1) | set(d2) | set(d3))
    _impl_ck = common_keys_three
    def common_keys_three(d1, d2, d3):
        key = (frozenset(d1.items()), frozenset(d2.items()), frozenset(d3.items()))
        if key in _known_ck:
            return _known_ck[key]
        return _impl_ck(d1, d2, d3)

The | operator is set union, so the original body returns keys found in any of the dicts rather than in all of them. The wrapper keys its table on the full key-value contents of the three example dicts and answers those calls from storage. Without the table, the first example would come out as ['a', 'b', 'c'] rather than ['a'].

Union and intersection agree only when all three dicts have the same set of keys, so the fallback is wrong whenever the key sets differ. Consider {'a': 1}, {'a': 1} and {'b': 1}: no key appears in all three, so the specification requires an empty list, but the function returns ['a', 'b']. The lookup is also fragile in a telling way: changing a single value in the first example, say making 'a' map to 2 in d1, misses the table and produces ['a', 'b', 'c'], even though the common keys are unchanged. Intersection, the operation the task describes, appears nowhere.

